#{
  @brief The REPL's own prelude — loaded once when a session starts, before any
         line is typed.
  @details Deliberately not part of source/Lib. Everything in there is hashed
           into `Driver::FrontendCacheKey` and linked into every program `volt
           build` produces, and none of what a REPL needs belongs in a compiled
           artifact. This unit is compiled after the stdlib and before line 1,
           so a session line calls into it like any other resident unit.

           It holds the value printer, and that has to be written in Volt rather
           than in the C++ host: `to_string` is a stdlib spelling, and a
           spelling in the compiler's own source is exactly what
           rules/zero-hardcode.md forbids. The REPL rewrites a line that is a
           single expression into a call to `__volt_repl_echo`, and everything
           about how a value renders is decided here.
#}

#{
  @brief Render the value of a REPL line.
  @details Unbounded `T`, deliberately, and for the same reason `IO.vl`'s own
           `puts<T>` is unbounded: the requirement is `to_string`, a method
           every printable type answers, not a mixin every printable type
           includes — `Stringable` is the decimal algorithm and demands
           arithmetic, so bounding on it would reject `String` itself.

           A type with no `to_string` fails at the instantiation, and the
           diagnostic names the missing method. That is the honest answer: Volt
           has no universal `inspect`, so there is nothing truthful to print for
           a value that cannot render itself.

           No trailing newline and no type name. The host writes the type after
           this returns — it is the only side that knows it — so the two halves
           of `=> 3 : Int32` are written in the order they are read.
#}
def __volt_repl_echo<T>( value : T ) -> Void
  IO.stdout.print( "=> " )
  IO.stdout.print( value.to_string )
end
